  Rinse the @French green lentils{1 1/2%cups} and put them in a #saucepan{} with plenty of lightly salted water, the @bay leaf{1}, and the @thyme{5%sprigs}. Bring to a boil, decrease the heat to a simmer, and cook for ~{15%minutes}. Add the @carrot{1}(peeled and finely diced), @red onion{1%small}(peeled and finely diced), and @celery{1%rib}(finely diced) and cook for another ~{5-10%minutes}, until the lentils are tender; be careful not to overcook them.

While the lentils are cooking, make the dressing. Mix the @red wine vinegar{1%tablespoon}, @sea salt{1 1/4%teaspoons}, @Dijon mustard{1%teaspoon}, @olive oil{1/3%cup}, and @shallot{1%small}(peeled and minced) in a #large bowl{}.

Drain the lentils well and mix them into the dressing while still warm, stirring to coat the lentils. Remove the bay leaf and thyme and let cool to room temperature, stirring occasionally.

Add a few grinds of @ground black pepper{} and mix in the @fresh flat-leaf parsley{1/2%cup}(finely chopped), @walnuts or pecans{1%cup}(toasted and coarsely chopped), and @goat cheese or feta cheese{1%cup}(crumbled). Taste, and add additional salt, if desired.

> I serve the salad at room temperature or warm. If served warm, omit the goat cheese, or crumble it on top at the last minute, so it doesn't melt—but instead, slightly softens.

> The salad can be made up to 2 days ahead and refrigerated. Let it come to room temperature before serving; it may need to be re-seasoned once refrigerated.